      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah, I know why... we sometimes generate code like this:\n\n```\n        if self.x.len() \u003e 0xff_ffff_ffff {\n            panic!(\"Invalid length for {}::{}: {} \u003e {}\", \"Bar\", \"x\", self.x.len(), 0xff_ffff_ffff);\n        }\n```\n\nThe first usage of `0xff_ffff_ffff` is fine since the comparison forces the type to be `usize`. The second usage is the problem: the free literal is inferred to be an `i32` and this type can be too small for the literal.\n\nHere, you _only_ use `max_value` in a context where the type will be correctly inferred, so not having a suffix will work. Using `\"u64\"` here should also work: that\u0027s a bit more consistent with the rest of the code since we use `u64` whenever we\u0027re masking a large PDL value and `usize` when we\u0027re masking a length.",
